<div class="callout"><%== icon(:info, size: 18) %><span>General information, not financial advice. Rates change often: check today's rates and the provider's own documents before you put money in.</span></div>

<div class="card prose">
  <h2>The order that makes sense</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Emergency fund first.</strong> Three to six months of living costs that you can reach within days. It stops a surprise bill from turning into a loan.</li>
    <li><strong>Clear expensive debt.</strong> A loan costing more than investments pay is the best "investment" to pay off.</li>
    <li><strong>Then invest</strong> what Future Me gets each month, matching each cedi to when you'll need it.</li>
  </ol>
</div>

<div class="card prose">
  <h2>Options in Ghana</h2>

  <h3>Money market funds</h3>
  <p>Unit trusts that hold T-bills and bank deposits. You can usually withdraw within a few days, and many accept MoMo top-ups, so they suit an emergency fund. Run by fund managers licensed by the SEC Ghana, for example Databank, IC Asset Managers and Stanbic Investment Management. Check the fees: they come out of your return.</p>

  <h3>Treasury bills</h3>
  <p>91-, 182- and 364-day loans to the government, auctioned every week by the Bank of Ghana. Buy through your bank, a licensed dealer or broker. You get the interest at maturity. T-bills were left out of the 2022–23 domestic debt exchange. When one matures, record what you received here and roll it over if you still don't need the money.</p>

  <h3>Government bonds</h3>
  <p>Two years or longer, paying interest twice a year. They can be sold before maturity on the Ghana Fixed Income Market, at whatever price buyers offer. The debt exchange cut what many bondholders were owed, so bonds are not risk-free.</p>

  <h3>Fixed deposits</h3>
  <p>A set rate for a set time at a bank or savings and loans company licensed by the Bank of Ghana. Deposits at member institutions are protected by the Ghana Deposit Protection Corporation up to a limit.</p>

  <h3>Shares on the Ghana Stock Exchange</h3>
  <p>For money you won't need for five years or more: prices go up and down, and some companies pay dividends. MTN Ghana is the largest listed company. You buy through a licensed broker and hold the shares in a Central Securities Depository account.</p>

  <h3>Tier 3 pension</h3>
  <p>Voluntary pension savings with a trustee licensed by the NPRA. Contributions within the legal limit reduce your income tax, which makes it worth more than its interest alone. Taking money out early can mean paying that tax back, so treat it as long-term money. Ask your employer or trustee about the current limit.</p>
</div>

<div class="card prose">
  <h2>Comparing options</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Beat inflation.</strong> A 15% return with 20% inflation loses value. The Ghana Statistical Service publishes inflation every month.</li>
    <li><strong>Know when you can get it out.</strong> Days, months or years? Don't lock away money you may need.</li>
    <li><strong>Count the fees</strong> and any tax.</li>
    <li><strong>Use the T-bill rate as a yardstick.</strong> Anything paying far more is taking far more risk.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="card prose">
  <h2>Red flags</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Guaranteed high returns, or returns far above the T-bill rate "with no risk". Menzgold and DKM promised that.</li>
    <li>Pressure to decide today, or to bring in friends and family.</li>
    <li>A firm you can't find on the official lists: the SEC Ghana (fund managers, brokers), the Bank of Ghana (banks, savings and loans, microfinance) and the NPRA (pension trustees).</li>
  </ul>
</div>

